MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b8c5f8bea1b438d4687ad9b712e77c3e, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level 1-10 End
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CutsceneData
  line:
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: 'In 1885, having completed the second of my two courses, with my credentials
      of licentiate in medicine and also in philosophy and literature, I made a trip
      through the country provinces to study the Spanish peasant, for the rural people,
      I thought, being agriculturists, would be most like the farmer folk of my native
      land. I just hope that my countrymen do not suffer in comparison, for the Spanish
      peasants had not greatly changed from the day when they were so masterfully
      described by '
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: "In the fall of '85, I found myself in Paris, studying art, visiting
      the various museums and associating with the Lunas, the Taveras and other Filipino
      residents of the French capital. In Paris a fourth of \u201CNoli Me Tangere\u201D
      was written, and I specialized in ophthalmology, devoting my attention to those
      eye troubles that were most prevalent in the Philippines and least understood."
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 2
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: My mother's growing blindness made me covet the skill which might enable
      me to restore her sight. So successfully did I study that I became the favorite
      pupil of Doctor L. de Weckert, the leading authority among the oculists of
      France, and author of a three-volume standard work. Around this time, I found
      myself very interested in Ferdinand Blumentritt's studies of the Philippines
      and wrote to him directly, as it housed genuine interest for the country, and
      not mere interest for a novelty project. Mr. Ferdinand wrote back, and we kept
      on exchanging letters regularly.
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 6
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: I then went to Germany, having continued my studies in its language
      in France, and was present at Heidelberg on the 500th anniversary of the foundation
      of the university. Since I had no passport, I could only attend a few lectures,
      but could not formally enroll myself in the university. I lived in one of the
      student boarding houses, with a number of law students, and was offered membership
      in the Chess Club. I accepted, and it allowed me to better understand the people
      of the town
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: From Heidelberg, to Leipzig, and a little later, to Berlin. I became
      acquainted with a number of men who had studied the Philippines and knew it
      as none whom he had ever met previously. Chief among these was Doctor Jagor,
      the author of the book which ten years before had inspired in him his life
      purpose of preparing his people for the time when America should come to the
      Philippines.
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 1
  - speakerName: Jose Rizal
    speakerType: 0
    hero: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 618ec32722d755e4e80a7f213a9d80b0, type: 2}
    enemy: {fileID: 0}
    Dialogue: Then there was Doctor Rudolf Virchow, head of the Anthropological Society
      and one of the greatest scientists in the world. Virchow was of intensely democratic
      ideals, he was a statesman as well as a scientist, and my interest in the history
      of his country and in everything else which concerned it, and his sincere earnestness,
      so intelligently directed toward helping his country, made Rizal at once a
      prime favorite.
    isLeftSide: 1
    pose: 2
